{"causes":["Living in exile in Basel, Switzerland during the late 19th century exposed Nietzsche to diverse influences and ideas that shaped his philosophy.","Nietzsche's experiences with mental illness and personal struggles deeply impacted his worldview and literary output.","The intellectual climate of late 19th-century Europe, characterized by debates over Darwinism, positivism, and nihilism, provided a fertile ground for Nietzsche's revolutionary ideas."],"effects":["The publication of Ecce Homo in 1888 marked a significant shift in Nietzsche's reputation from respected academic to provocative provocateur.","Nietzsche's critique of traditional morality and Christianity resonated with the growing disillusionment among young intellectuals during the fin-de-siècle era.","The essay's emphasis on individualism, self-overcoming, and perspectivalism influenced the development of existentialist thought in the early 20th century."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"friedrich-nietzsche","sourceName":"Friedrich Nietzsche","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"ecce-homo","targetName":"Ecce Homo","context":"Nietzsche published Ecce Homo in 1888."},{"sourceSlug":"basel-switzerland","sourceName":"Basel, Switzerland","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ecce-homo","targetName":"Ecce Homo","context":"Nietzsche's exile in Basel exposed him to diverse influences."},{"sourceSlug":"darwinism","sourceName":"Darwinism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ecce-homo","targetName":"Ecce Homo","context":"The intellectual climate of late 19th-century Europe, characterized by debates over Darwinism, provided a fertile ground for Nietzsche's revolutionary ideas."},{"sourceSlug":"existentialism","sourceName":"Existentialism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ecce-homo","targetName":"Ecce Homo","context":"Nietzsche's emphasis on individualism and perspectivalism resonated with the development of existentialist thought in the early 20th century."},{"sourceSlug":"martin-heidegger","sourceName":"Martin Heidegger","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"ecce-homo","targetName":"Ecce Homo","context":"Heidegger drew upon Nietzsche's ideas in his own philosophical works."},{"sourceSlug":"jean-paul-sartre","sourceName":"Jean-Paul Sartre","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"ecce-homo","targetName":"Ecce Homo","context":"Sartre's existentialism was heavily influenced by Nietzsche's critique of traditional morality and Christianity."},{"sourceSlug":"michel-foucault","sourceName":"Michel Foucault","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"ecce-homo","targetName":"Ecce Homo","context":"Foucault's poststructuralist thought was shaped by Nietzsche's rejection of traditional knowledge structures."},{"sourceSlug":"postmodernism","sourceName":"Postmodernism","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"ecce-homo","targetName":"Ecce Homo","context":"Nietzsche's critique of modernity and his emphasis on perspectivalism prefigured key aspects of postmodern thought."}],"places":[{"name":"Basel, Switzerland","role":"Exile"},{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Publication site"},{"name":"Weimar Republic, Germany","role":"Cultural hub"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Ecce Homo","type":"Reference article","slug":"ecce-homo"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q861527","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecce_Homo_(book)"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Ecce Homo contributed to the historical record of the Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Ecce Homo: How One Becomes What One Is (German: Ecce Homo: Wie man wird, was man ist) is the autobiographical work of Friedrich Nietzsche (1844–1900), written in a burst of extraordinary energy in October 1888 — two to three months before his mental collapse in January 1889 in Turin — and published posthumously in 1908, more than a decade after his breakdown. In Ecce Homo, Nietzsche reviews his own philosophical development and his major works (The Birth of Tragedy, Human All Too Human, Thus Spoke Zarathustra, Beyond Good and Evil, On the Genealogy of Morality, Twilight of the Idols, The Antichrist, The Case of Wagner) with a mixture of philosophical clarity, extraordinary self-appreciation ('Why I Am So Wise', 'Why I Am So Clever', 'Why I Write Such Good Books'), and moments of grandiosity that retrospectively raise the question of whether the mental illness that would consume him was already affecting his writing.

Ecce Homo is simultaneously Nietzsche's clearest and most accessible self-presentation — the chapters on his individual works contain some of his most lucid explanations of his philosophical aims — and his most difficult and disturbing text. The combination of genuine philosophical insight, remarkable self-awareness, bitter polemic (particularly against Wagner and German culture), and megalomaniacal self-aggrandisement makes Ecce Homo one of the strangest and most unsettling documents in the history of Western philosophy: a philosopher's last self-portrait on the threshold of madness.

The title is the Latin phrase Pontius Pilate used when presenting the flagellated Jesus to the Jerusalem crowd — 'Behold the man!' — and Nietzsche's use of it as the title of his philosophical autobiography is characteristically provocative and multilayered: an identification of himself as a man to be looked upon, a secular replacement for Christ, and an allusion to the kind of fate his philosophical work might bring upon him. Nietzsche had already written in a letter that he was 'dynamite' — that his philosophy would fundamentally transform Western thought — and Ecce Homo is in part his attempt to control the interpretation of his explosive legacy.","subjects":["Philosophy","Modern Era","Nietzsche","Autobiography","19th Century","Continental Philosophy","German Literature","Philosophy of Self"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Nietzsche's Ecce Homo is a seminal work that challenged traditional morality and Christianity, influencing existentialist thought and shaping the development of postmodern philosophy.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
